Input: {"question": "In which city is the Washington Street station located?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 0, "end": 179, "text": "Washington Street station (MBTA) Washington Street is a surface stop on the Massachusetts Bay Transportation Authority (MBTA)'s Green Line \"B\" Branch, located in Brighton, Boston."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 180, "end": 271, "text": "The station is located in the median of Commonwealth Avenue northeast of Washington Street."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 272, "end": 370, "text": "Washington Street station consists of two side platforms, which serve the \"B\" Branch's two tracks."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 371, "end": 415, "text": "The station is fully handicapped accessible."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 416, "end": 520, "text": "From October 27, 1926 to January 23, 1953, a passing siding was in place just east of Washington Street."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 521, "end": 567, "text": "It was occasionally used to short turn trains."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d00", "start": 568, "end": 665, "text": "Washington Street is the fifth-busiest \"B\" Branch surface station (after Harvard Avenue, Packards"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d01", "start": 0, "end": 22, "text": "– via Newspapers.com ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d01", "start": 23, "end": 39, "text": "^ Plotkin , A.S."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0075-bae5d4cee61b:d01", "start": 40, "end": 60, "text": "( March 1 , 1974 ) ."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdoc